{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — Cart note
  Phase 14. Shopify's own cart note, and nothing beside it.

  Accepts:
  - id      {String} required. The textarea's id. Two cart surfaces can render
                     this, so it is namespaced by the caller for the same
                     reason snippets/cart-line-item.liquid namespaces its own.
  - form_id {String} optional. Only needed if the caller places this outside
                     the cart form; both current callers place it inside.

  Usage:
  {% render 'cart-note', id: 'CartDrawerNote' %}

  Two consumers — the drawer and the cart page — which is why it is a snippet.

  IT IS ONE FIELD NAMED note, BECAUSE THAT IS THE WHOLE FEATURE.
  Shopify's cart carries a single free-text note, posted as `note` by the cart
  form and settable through /cart/update.js. There is nothing here to design:
  a second field, a dropdown of reasons or a "gift message" variant would all
  be inventing store policy that nobody supplied, and the Phase 14 brief rules
  out exactly that — "If not required: do not add a large unnecessary field."

  So it is off by default, and the merchant turns it on per surface.

  "ORDER NOTE", NOT "SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS".
  The brief names the label and names the wording it does not want. Both
  strings live in the locale file, so neither is written in markup.

  A <details>, FOR THE DRAWER'S SAKE.
  A permanently open textarea costs about 120px, which in a 420px drawer is
  taken from the cart lines — the part the customer came for. Collapsed, the
  note is one row of text until it is wanted.

  This is safe because of a fact Phase 13 established and tested: form controls
  inside a CLOSED <details> are still submitted. Collapsing the note never
  discards it. It is rendered open when the cart already carries one, so an
  existing note is never hidden behind a control the customer has to find.

  And it is a native disclosure — no ARIA, no script — which is the same
  reasoning sections/main-product.liquid records for its description and
  snippets/facets.liquid for its filter groups.
{%- endcomment -%}

<details class="cart-note" {% if cart.note != blank %}open{% endif %}>
  <summary class="cart-note__summary">
    <span class="cart-note__summary-label">{{ 'cart.note.label' | t }}</span>
    {% render 'icon-chevron' %}
  </summary>

  <div class="cart-note__body">
    {%- comment -%}
      A real <label>, not a placeholder. A placeholder is not an accessible
      name, and it disappears the moment the customer starts typing — which is
      precisely when they most need to know what the field is for.

      The label is visually hidden because the summary above it already carries
      the same words: shown, the field would read "Order note / Order note".
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <label class="visually-hidden" for="{{ id }}">{{ 'cart.note.label' | t }}</label>
    {%- comment -%}
      PHASE 17: cart.note IS ESCAPED.

      Liquid does not escape output, and a </textarea> inside the note closes
      this element early — verified by rendering a note of
      "</textarea><img src=x onerror=...>", which produced a live element after
      the field. The note is customer-supplied and is also settable through
      /cart/update.js, so it is untrusted input however it arrived.

      escape, not escape_once: the stored value is plain text, and escape_once
      would leave a literal "&amp;" typed by a customer looking like an entity.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <textarea
      class="cart-note__field"
      id="{{ id }}"
      name="note"
      rows="3"
      data-cart-note
      {% if form_id != blank %}form="{{ form_id }}"{% endif %}
    >{{ cart.note | escape }}</textarea>

    <p class="cart-note__help">{{ 'cart.note.help' | t }}</p>
  </div>
</details>
